1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Everybody plants a tree.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Nobody finds out what happened to it.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A birthday.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A school drive.

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A company afternoon.

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
They plant it, they take the photo, they post it,

7
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
and that is where it ends.

8
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
The planting is an event.

9
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The keeping alive is a year.

10
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
So how much of it lives?

11
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
India's national auditor went and looked.

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Of a two point eight million hectare target,

13
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
about five percent was achieved.

14
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
At seventy percent of the sites,

15
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
no improvement in tree cover at all.

16
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
One site reported two thousand plantings.

17
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Thirty were found.

18
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,000
None had survived.

19
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
And here is the part that explains the rest.

20
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Four out of five plantation records did not say what was

21
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
planted, where it stood, or whether it lived.

22
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Nobody was ever asked to keep one.

23
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Planting is not the solution.

24
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Keeping it alive is.

25
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
And we already pay people to do boring things.

26
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Pay a credit card bill on time, and you earn points.

27
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Water the tree you planted last year,

28
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
and nothing happens at all.

29
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Rooted pays you for keeping it alive.

30
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Register a plant once.

31
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Do the small jobs it needs, photographed as you do them.

32
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
You do not have to know anything about the plant.

33
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Every plant carries its own coordinates,

34
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
and the schedule moves with the weather over it.

35
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Rain pushes the next watering out.

36
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Heat pulls it in.

37
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
So nobody has to babysit it.

38
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
It tells you the job on the day it is due.

39
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Water, on this species own interval,

40
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
moved by the rain and the heat.

41
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Feeding, with what this plant wants, and less than you think,

42
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
because what the roots do not take ends up in the groundwater.

43
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Pests, with what goes wrong with this species,

44
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
and the mildest thing that works.

45
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
And a weekly look while it establishes,

46
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
which is when losing it is least visible.

47
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Every plant also carries a health score.

48
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Thriving, steady, watch it, at risk,

49
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and one sentence saying what moved it.

50
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
It does not read the leaves and call the plant sick.

51
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A yellow leaf has a dozen causes.

52
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The camera then tells you what would make the shot pass.

53
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Out loud,

54
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
because whoever is doing this is holding a watering can.

55
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
It reads the frame twice a second, on the phone,

56
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,000
uploading nothing.

57
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Tilt down so the soil is in frame.

58
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Then the ring turns green.

59
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Then it is checked, and every check says the number it measured.

60
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,000
It came off the camera.

61
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The time is ours.

62
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The location matches where this plant was registered.

63
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
And soil five percent darker than that plant's own dry baseline.

64
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Measured, not guessed.

65
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
This is the one that matters.

66
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
If any plant earned points, the points would be worth nothing.

67
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
So OpenCV matches keypoints against the plant's first

68
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
photograph, and asks whether they agree on one viewpoint.

69
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The same plant returns four hundred and eighty five.

70
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
A different plant of the same species, in a similar pot,

71
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,000
returns four.

72
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Registering a plant checks the photograph before it accepts it.

73
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Point it at a notebook and it says so.

74
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
An object scores nought point two percent.

75
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
The weakest real plant scored nine.

76
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Point it at a plant and it works out what it is.

77
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Seventy-eight species, searched by local name.

78
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
The whole premise is that people forget.

79
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
So this cannot wait to be opened.

80
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
When a task comes due, the same message goes out on WhatsApp,

81
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
email and text.

82
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
A name, a plant and a date go to the server.

83
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
No photographs, no coordinates.

84
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Organisations spend heavily on planting drives,

85
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
and get back a photograph from day one.

86
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Rooted produces the record of what survived,

87
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
every point traceable to one verified task.

88
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
That record funds the rewards.

89
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
The offers are stand-ins,

90
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
because naming a real company would put words in their mouth.

91
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Two things did not survive contact with a measurement.

92
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
I tried to work out the species on the device.

93
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
It got the right answer zero times out of seventeen.

94
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And the identity check used to compare brightness grids.

95
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
A different neem tree walked straight through.

96
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Against eight percent for guessing.

97
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
It was the weakest thing in the app wearing the name of the

98
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,000
strongest, so it was replaced.

99
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
We reward people for spending money.

100
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Rooted rewards them for keeping something alive.

101
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Plant it.

102
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Keep it.

103
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Get paid for it.

104
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Nothing in here reports a number it did not measure.